#@MNX-CHUNK id=03.00 slug=storage-invariants title="Non-negotiable storage and data invariants"
#######################################################################################################
# SECTION 03 — NON-NEGOTIABLE STORAGE AND DATA INVARIANTS
#######################################################################################################
#
# Authoritative paths:
#
#   m16 live /home                         /home
#   m16 local btrbk snapshots              /home/.btrbk-snapshots
#   fast backup NVMe                       /mnt/m16-backup-nvme
#   fast received /home history            /mnt/m16-backup-nvme/btrbk-snapshots
#   128 GiB overflow swap                   /mnt/m16-backup-nvme/swap/swapfile
#   authoritative new-storage HDD          /mnt/m16-new-storage
#   long-term m16 archive                   /mnt/m16-new-storage/admin/backups
#   m21 received snapshots                  /mnt/m16-new-storage/syncs/m21
#   m21 Restic repository                    /mnt/m16-new-storage/admin/backups/restic/m21
#   old-storage browseable mirror          /mnt/m16-old-storage
#
# OLD-STORAGE LAW:
#
#   /mnt/m16-old-storage is mounted read-only in the host namespace. The only enabled file-content job permitted
#   to obtain a writable view is rsync-m16-new-to-old.service, after it owns /run/storage-maintenance.lock. That
#   unit gets a private mount namespace, remounts only its private old-storage view read-write, performs the
#   browseable mirror, synchronizes, and restores that private view read-only on success, failure, cancellation,
#   or timeout. The host mount remains read-only throughout. Do not add scrub, dedupe, archive, m21, test,
#   temporary receive, support, or housekeeping writes there. The existing
#   /mnt/m16-old-storage/admin/backups/emergency-m21 tree remains outside the mirror's managed content because
#   /admin/backups/*** is excluded.
#
# Native Btrfs histories under new-storage are not browseable-rsync payloads and are not duperemove targets:
#
#   /mnt/m16-new-storage/admin/backups
#   /mnt/m16-new-storage/syncs/m21
#
# The shared heavy-storage lock is /run/storage-maintenance.lock. An operator test/switch queues for and then owns
# it before classifying maintenance. Scheduled lock users make one nonblocking attempt and leave a clean skipped
# product for the freshness controller; they never queue behind or overtake an operator transaction. Root-filesystem
# scrub remains exclusive. The throttled new-storage scrub and online fstrim remain visible contention only.
#
# STORAGE IS A DATA CONTRACT BEFORE IT IS AN IMPLEMENTATION CHOICE. A path, subvolume, repository and mirror
# are not interchangeable just because each contains bytes. Record which data is authoritative, which copy is
# independent, what its recovery scope is, and which process may mutate it. A source rebuild does not restore
# missing user data. A filesystem snapshot is not by itself an independently stored backup.
#
# FUTURE CLEANUP RULE: identify ownership, active references, recoverability and retained alternatives before
# removing residue. Disable obsolete execution separately from deleting its history. Existing accepted retention
# remains implemented policy; this note schedules no deletion, repository unlock or retention change.
#
#@MNX-CHUNK id=04.00 slug=backup-policy-office title="Backup relationships and current policy"
#######################################################################################################
# SECTION 04 — BACKUP RELATIONSHIPS AND CURRENT POLICY
#######################################################################################################
#
#   /home
#     -> local read-only btrbk snapshots
#     -> daily incremental receive on backup NVMe
#     -> weekly long-retention Btrfs archive on new-storage
#
#   REQUIRED M21 RECOVERY OUTCOME (h0p3, 2026-09-06):
#     /home/h0p3 and /home/overseer are the treasures; protect their valuable nested data too.
#     Also retain /etc/nixos/configuration.nix and local Nix inputs needed for reconstruction.
#     /nix/store package binaries are not required. This names scope; it does not certify current coverage.
#
#   comfy-m21 /home — MANDATORY PRIMARY: RESTIC
#     -> transient read-only source snapshot and source export
#     -> m16-initiated pinned SSH as backup-source@192.168.100.119
#     -> local Restic ingest under /mnt/m16-new-storage/admin/backups/restic/m21
#     -> source cleanup receipt, completed-backup receipt, m16-only maintenance and retention
#     -> condition-gated scheduling; observed enrollment is not a newly performed restore test
#
#   comfy-m21 /home — PRESERVED LEGACY: BTRFS PULL
#     -> wired endpoint 192.168.100.119, with Wi-Fi fallback 192.168.1.25
#     -> separate helper trust and direct receive under /mnt/m16-new-storage/syncs/m21
#     -> history and manual service retained, timer masked; its helper mismatch does not block Restic
#
# These are parallel implementations, not a serial path through Btrfs receive and then Restic. The known
# Restic scope is the existing home export. Foundation configuration, other mounts, nested datasets and
# application-consistent recovery need a named coverage inventory before anyone calls this a whole-machine backup.
#
#   ordinary new-storage files
#     -> monthly rsync browseable mirror on read-only-by-default old-storage
#     -> private writable remount exists only inside the locked rsync unit
#     -> excludes /admin/backups/*** and /syncs/m21/***
#
# Freshness catch-up priority and thresholds:
#
#   1. btrbk-main                         36 hours
#   2. m16-restic-m21                    nightly 03:25; retry from 04:00; deadline 07:00 America/New_York
#   3. btrbk-archive-to-new-storage       9 days
#   4. rsync-m16-new-to-old              35 days
#
# Restic is the mandatory m21 lane. The old Btrfs helper block applies only to the preserved legacy lane.
# The legacy timer is explicitly masked in Stage85. All Btrfs/Restic data are preserved; no data deletion is authorized.
# Start no more than one due job per catch-up evaluation, every 30 minutes after the existing 45-minute
# activation grace. Least-recently-dispatched due jobs win, with the listed priority breaking ties; a failing
# m21 cannot starve m16 home backup and vice versa. Duperemove is never an essential catch-up job.
# Nightly proof uses the successful attempt's start as a conservative source-time bound. Completion alone
# cannot make an old source current. A missed 07:00 deadline is critical backup evidence, not disk damage
# and not permission to disable local configuration repair. Repository restore proof remains independent.
# A lock-busy or temporarily unreachable m21 is a clean skip; damaged lineage, identity mismatch, I/O error,
# bad Received UUID, counter increase, or a real rsync failure is not a clean skip.
#
# BACKUP THEORY TO MAKE EXECUTABLE IN SMALL STEPS: the product is a recoverable dataset at a stated source
# time, not a successful timer. Separate source coverage, transfer completion, source cleanup, repository
# checking and restoration witnesses. Give every obligation a window, deadline and honest unavailable state.
# A cached observer can report these facts without contacting the source on every local configuration audit.
# A fresh live network probe is a distinct operational action, not an implicit acceptance dependency.
#
# Restic stays the primary m21 mechanism. Favor repairing and simplifying its already functioning path over
# another backend rewrite. Preserve the old recovery points until explicit verification justifies retirement;
# no planned simplification should make tonight's successful copy harder to obtain.
#
#@MNX-CHUNK id=05.00 slug=incident-memory title="Incident memory and known traps"
#######################################################################################################
# SECTION 05 — INCIDENT MEMORY: DO NOT RELEARN THESE THE HARD WAY
#######################################################################################################
#
# * The 128 GiB disk swap belongs on the backup NVMe, not /mnt/torrents.
# * A large Btrfs swapfile can take a long time to allocate. A long mkswap unit is not automatically frozen.
# * Btrfs receive creates read-only received subvolumes. Do not receive into a temporary parent and `mv` the
#   received subvolume afterward; preserving received_uuid requires direct receive into the final parent.
# * The m21 source once had checksum-corrupt live extents. A clean, complete data-bearing helper send was the
#   certification gate. Current pulls must continue verifying read-only state, Received UUID, parent lineage,
#   helper identity, host identity, and unchanged destination counters.
# * Generated systemd scripts have restricted PATHs. Every utility they invoke, including awk/gawk, must be in
#   the declared service path or referenced through an explicit Nix-store path.
# * Nix derivation checks and root activation scripts may run with HOME unset. A generated program using `set -u`
#   must not dereference HOME during top-level setup before its nonmutating policy/self-test path can run; derivation
#   and installed-runtime gates must exercise that path under `env -u HOME`. Real vault commands still require a
#   normal user HOME or both explicit cipher and mount paths.
# * `environment.etc` normally projects immutable store files into /etc through root-owned symlinks. Runtime audits
#   must validate the symlink chain and resolved bounded root-owned nonwritable /nix/store file instead of demanding
#   a copied regular /etc pathname or trusting an arbitrary link target.
# * The configuration-as-Bash bootstrap is run by an older already-active generation while testing a newer source.
#   Therefore its embedded payload must establish its own PATH and may not assume the candidate generation is active.
# * A valid `nixos-rebuild test` baseline may itself be a temporary running closure. The test harness must preserve
#   that exact prior running closure for rollback and separately prove that the boot-default profile did not change.
# * Downloading, Sublime copy/paste, `cp`, or `mv` may preserve or discard source ownership and executable mode.
#   Candidate text is authoritative; `mnx test` must not depend on mode 0755. It freezes an exact root-owned candidate
#   snapshot before evaluation, and activation restores /etc/nixos/configuration.nix to 0755 only as a convenience.
# * Never execute the immutable Nix-store `sudo` binary directly. Privileged operator commands must use the setuid
#   wrapper at /run/wrappers/bin/sudo. A store-path sudo exists as program data but cannot perform escalation itself.
# * The embedded candidate bootstrap must test its own embedded implementation rather than delegating to an older
#   installed mnx-test. Otherwise the precise bootstrap bug being repaired can prevent the repairing candidate test.
# * Source-audit tools belong to the active generation. If that active auditor is itself broken, a different candidate
#   cannot repair it through ordinary `mnx test`; candidate-source changes must use the source's embedded bootstrap,
#   build-check the candidate-owned tools, activate temporarily, run the candidate postflight, and roll back on failure.
# * Background storage work has three classes. Jobs that already own the shared mutation lock and root-filesystem
#   scrub are bounded exclusions; operator test/switch acquires that lock first and continuously proves source, closure,
#   and health identity while waiting. Scheduled lock users make one nonblocking attempt so they cannot overtake a queued
#   operator. Online fstrim and the 50 MiB/s new-storage scrub are contention-only and never require h0p3 to poll.
# * The incident scanner once matched its own warning and created a feedback loop. Never let an observer treat
#   its own bundle-written or unit-name messages as fresh incidents.
# * A historical Prometheus textfile is indistinguishable from current state unless its semantics and timestamp
#   say otherwise. Current job metrics now use `monsternix_job_current_*`; unit failure history lives in logbook.
# * The old rsync log once ended in `done` after rsync code 23. A trailing word is not proof of success. Legacy
#   bootstrap must parse an entire run and reject any error-bearing run.
# * Nix indented strings strip only their minimum common indentation. A single column-zero line can leave every other
#   line indented in the rendered script, silently breaking heredoc terminators. Generated programs must be checked
#   after Nix rendering during the derivation build; source-only extraction is not enough.
# * python-xlib and python3-xlib both import as Xlib but do not share the same API. Record the distribution
#   used by local tests; python-xlib get_best_auth compares byte addresses, not str hostnames. A passing test
#   against a substitute dependency does not prove the Nix-selected dependency passed.
# * A checksum manifest must never hash itself. Build SHA256SUMS outside the bundle directory, exclude the final
#   manifest name, atomically move it into place, and verify it before committing `.done` or incident cooldown state.
# * Btrfs device counters are cumulative evidence. Compare mount UUID + device + counter-kind tuples against a pinned
#   baseline; unchanged historical nonzero values are advisory, increases are failures, and a summed total is invalid.
# * Permanent activation must use the root-owned frozen candidate named by the newest completed successful test for the
#   exact current source hash. Never let `mnx switch` re-evaluate mutable editor text or channels after testing; promote the exact tested system closure.
# * Dispatcher helper names are part of the operator API. The Stage-11 seal exposed `latest_test` versus
#   `latest_completed_test`; generated dispatcher self-tests must exercise test and switch summary paths.
#
# * Source time, completion time, observation time and deadline are different quantities. Never compare them
#   as if a later timestamp proves a newer backup. Compare like bases and state unknown when provenance differs.
# * A nightly obligation is a dated window. Before today's window opens, previous-night coverage is not proof
#   that tonight's scheduled run already succeeded. Configuration acceptance is not runtime soak evidence.
# * Copy-paste is the operator interface. An interactive shell's $0 may be Bash, not a saved script path.
#   A bridge must consume its supplied text explicitly and prove its launcher before requesting elevation.
# * A shared local prompt lock needs observation, not writable access. Preserve the read-only-home sandbox;
#   test the actual descriptor mode and lock exclusion rather than weakening permissions after a refusal.
# * A trusted publication proves where evidence came from, not every claim someone might attach to it.
#   Snapshot headers are not restored data; a helper hash is not semantic review; a PASS label is not a proof.
#
#@MNX-CHUNK id=06.00 slug=thermal-swap-oom title="Thermal, swap, and OOM reality"
#######################################################################################################
# SECTION 06 — THERMAL, SWAP, AND OOM REALITY
#######################################################################################################
#
# Historical evidence includes a warm backup NVMe reaching the low 70s Celsius during sustained I/O. h0p3
# reports adding a fan aimed at it. The Stage81 2026-09-05 02:32:37 EDT health sample reported 36C/53C and
# zero critical-warning values. That sample is evidence, not a causal estimate of fan benefit or a load test.
# Preserve visibility: 70–79C remains an advisory warning and 80C remains critical. Do not automatically stop
# correct backup work merely because the drive is in the accepted warning band when other health checks clear.
#
# The officer owns ordinary hardware sensing and interpretation. Correlate temperatures with workload,
# sample age, sensor/device identity, I/O pressure and counter deltas before changing policy. A storage warning
# caused by overdue backup evidence is not overheating; an absolute temperature swing may describe cooling.
# Keep missing sensors and stale observations explicit. Do not reset hardware counters to manufacture health.
#
# Swap design is intentional:
#
#   zram       priority 100, fast first tier
#   128 GiB    priority 10, backup-NVMe overflow tier
#
# The operator uses swap and has previously exhausted memory despite substantial capacity. OOM policy should
# preserve responsiveness without pretending disk swap is disposable or unused.
#
# DIRECTION: a small workload-aware record should be more useful than a second monitoring empire. Collection
# cost, retained bytes, missed-sample behavior and the question answered belong beside each future sensor.
# Stage83 preserves hardware collection, OOM tuning, cooling controls, thresholds and sampling intervals.
#
#@MNX-CHUNK id=07.00 slug=logging-support-contract title="Logging, metrics, and future-ChatGPT support contract"
#######################################################################################################
# SECTION 07 — LOGGING, METRICS, AND FUTURE-CHATGPT SUPPORT CONTRACT
#######################################################################################################
#
# Bare `mnx` is the canonical read-only identity-first wake action. `mnx test` is the canonical pre-switch evaluation
# command and `mnx switch` is the canonical permanent-activation command. h0p3 runs those two explicit phases; future
# officers must hand them off directly. `mnx apply` remains optional compatibility machinery rather than the default
# instruction. The larger command family exists for ChatGPT-directed diagnostics, recovery, and refactor work. After a candidate configuration.nix is copied into
# place, it accepts an ordinary non-executable copy, freezes the exact tested text beside the transcript, source-audits
# that frozen candidate, and saves every raw phase log beneath /var/lib/logbook/support. Normal success output is
# compact; complete logs remain available through `mnx test-raw`. Failure output includes bounded error context and
# diagnostics. A full support report runs by default only on failure. `--verbose --support=always` is an explicit opt-in.
#
# Ctrl-C, TERM, and HUP are first-class outcomes, not fake rebuild failures. The harness launches each long child in
# its own process group, records live state for `mnx test-status`, supports `mnx test-cancel`, terminates descendants,
# and rolls back only when activation actually began. Cancelling optional diagnostics after core validation preserves
# a successful core result. A hard SIGKILL cannot run traps; `mnx test-recover` verifies and terminates an orphan child
# process group from persisted runtime state, then restores the exact pre-test closure if activation may have occurred.
# An identical candidate is source/runtime audited without another rebuild.
#
# `mnx switch` will not rebuild arbitrary live text. It locates the newest completed test transcript for the exact
# current source hash, verifies the root-owned transcript, frozen candidate, and exact tested NixOS closure, then permanently promotes that closure, then proves running/default/source identity and runs the normal post-switch audit. It serializes against
# `mnx test` and storage maintenance. On activation or postflight failure it restores the prior boot-default profile
# and running closure. When `mnx test --allow-behavior-change` explicitly approved a reviewed behavior difference,
# successful `mnx switch` adopts the new repeatable behavior baseline; otherwise a mismatch is a hard failure.
#
# The baseline may be either the boot-default closure or an already-temporary but healthy running closure. If
# activation or postflight fails, it records evidence and restores that exact prior running closure by default while
# separately proving that the boot-default profile stayed unchanged.
#
# `mnx-support-report` is the default ordinary paste-back command. It must remain bounded, diagnostic-only, and
# explicit about every place it writes. It may save under /var/lib/logbook/support; it must not touch backup
# filesystems.
#
# Current state and history are different products:
#
#   /var/lib/node-exporter-textfile       current scrapeable state; root-only writers
#   /var/lib/monsternix/job-state/authoritative  root-only freshness/failure authority
#   /var/lib/monsternix/job-state/telemetry      group-writable non-authoritative last-run telemetry
#   /var/lib/logbook/unit-failures        historical unit-failure evidence
#   /var/lib/logbook/metrics-history      retired legacy metric snapshots
#
# The editing officer may replace any logging/observability mechanism declared in this monolith—including journald
# settings, namespaces, retention, evidence schemas, metrics, notifications, report shapes, and observer code—when a
# better sense organ is needed. This is an affirmative standing grant, not an emergency exception. The replacement
# must remain bounded, source-owned, explicit about destructive scope and authority, useful to both h0p3 and an
# amnesiac successor, and accepted through the ordinary test/switch transaction. Historical observer design is evidence.
#
# Reports should summarize noisy logs by default and expose raw tails only through an explicit opt-in. Bare `mnx`
# should fit the primary officer handoff on one screen; `mnx brief --full`, JSON, office, and help provide progressive
# disclosure. Future ChatGPT should be able to request one command, receive bounded output, and identify generation, policy, jobs,
# freshness, lineage, counters, SMART, swap, recent failures, and exact installed scripts.
# `mnx map` prints the chunk index; `mnx chunk ID` prints one exact source region; `mnx search REGEX` returns
# chunk-aware line locations; and `mnx humpty bundle` produces an uploadable full-source-plus-chunks archive.
#
# EVIDENCE SHOULD BECOME A SMALL, TYPED VOCABULARY, NOT A WALL OF GREEN TEXT. A useful claim names its
# subject, source identity, observation time, time basis, scope, result and limitation. A collector can be
# healthy while its subject is broken. An unknown or missed obligation should stay visible without making
# the machine impossible to repair. Contradictions are work items, not values to average into success.
#
# Timestamp discipline is implemented in the backup summary: retain completion age, label the freshness
# basis, compare like times and share one Restic receipt sample. Stage83 adds policy provenance: the same
# evaluated clock/cadence record drives native timers, the pure classifier and the public contract.
# The broader evidence vocabulary remains direction, not a claim of universal implementation. Do not add
# a parser for every sentence here; earn new schema fields through consumers and independent failure tests.
#
