# Stable historical chunk IDs are compatibility handles, not a promise that numeric sorting alone reconstructs
# the document. The manifest order is authoritative. In particular, the historical `20.20` firmware/boot
# foothold now lives physically inside Section 10. Machine-readable footholds use these column-zero forms:
#
#   #@MNX-CHUNK id=NN.NN slug=short-name title="Human title"
#   #@MNX-DEF path=attribute.path section=NN.NN role=service ordinal=NNN
#   #@MNX-PART id=stable-part-id kind=function title="Human title"
#   #@MNX-FLOW id=stable-flow kind=backup title="Human title" source="A" destination="B" defs='path.one,path.two'
#   #@MNX-LAW id=stable-law severity=hard title="Human title" statement="Invariant" defs='path.one,path.two'
#   #@MNX-OFFICE key=value
#   #@MNX-ZONE id=stable-zone interfaces=enp4s0 address=lan bind_policy=firewall-gated sources=192.168.100.0/24 enforcement=observe-only
#   #@MNX-EXPOSURE id=stable-id proto=tcp port=22 address=wildcard interfaces=* unit=sshd.service disposition=known ...
#
# CHUNK markers identify subsystem-sized exact split/join boundaries. DEF markers identify active top-level
# assignments. PART markers identify internal functions, phases, classes, and categories inside the largest
# definitions without splitting their liftable Nix blocks. FLOW and LAW records are centralized in Section 09.10:
# they link those exact definitions into operational data paths and enforceable project invariants.
#
# Section 98 is a candidate-only experiment bay. Paired #@MNX-TEMP markers may wrap active test code
# there. `mnx test` may activate such code temporarily and labels the result, but `mnx switch` refuses
# every candidate with an active temporary region. Promotion requires moving the code to its real section,
# removing the temporary markers, and running `mnx test` again. Future ChatGPT commands include `mnx atlas`,
# `mnx parts`, `mnx flows`, `mnx laws`, `mnx explain`, `mnx health`, `mnx exposure review`, `mnx exposure zones`, and `mnx temp status`; the operator still needs only
# `mnx test` and `mnx switch`.
#
# CHUNK markers delimit exact byte ranges for split/join/bundle work. DEF markers sit immediately before every
# active top-level assignment. `path=` is the stable definition identity; `section=` connects it to a chunk;
# `role=` gives a coarse operational class; `ordinal=` is only the current physical reading order. Search the
# complete marker or the path, then lift from the following assignment through its top-level terminating semicolon.
#
# The generated `mnx-monolith` tool validates chunk uniqueness, splits exact bytes, emits per-chunk hashes and a
# JSON manifest, rejoins edited derived chunks, and proves exact no-edit roundtrips. Definition anchors are inert
# comments, so older installed MNX tooling remains forward-compatible. They make the existing `mnx search` and
# ordinary ctrl+f useful at assignment granularity without creating a module/import system or a second source tree.
#
# Inside a mature subsystem, prefer this visible order:
#
#   Policy
#   Paths and constants
#   Helpers
#   Service implementation
#   Timer
#   Monitoring
#   Failure behavior
#   Rescue overrides
#   Operator commands
#
# Keep each generated Bash program complete in one visible Nix block. Shared helpers are fine when their API is
# obvious and the caller remains liftable. Never hide ordinary control flow behind abstraction merely to save
# lines. This document may grow very large. Correctness, provenance, copyability, and operator recognition win.
#
# AN EXTRACTABLE SUBSYSTEM SHOULD CARRY ITS CONTEXT. The long-term unit of reuse is a small packet of
# purpose, explicit inputs, implementation, permitted effects, evidence schema, tests and recovery notes.
# Today the CHUNK/DEF/PART/FLOW/LAW map supplies navigation and exact-source identity; it is not yet a
# universal package manager or cross-platform compiler. Keep implementation, machine policy and proof
# separately recognizable so another operator can lift an idea without inheriting h0p3's addresses or risks.
#
# FUTURE ADAPTER SEAM: policy says what must hold; the adapter says how this machine attempts it; observations
# say what occurred. Portable evidence can outlive an adapter without claiming the old binary runs everywhere.
# A compiled helper may be worthwhile where it simplifies a real protocol or safety boundary. It is not
# worthwhile merely to move the same complexity behind an opaque executable or to make all tests self-agree.
#
#@MNX-CHUNK id=09.08 slug=network-exposure-policy title="Canonical listener identity, network zones, and source-declared exposure intent"
#######################################################################################################
# SECTION 09.08 — CANONICAL NETWORK EXPOSURE IDENTITY AND ZONE POLICY
#######################################################################################################
#
# This is still observation and review—not a firewall allowlist. Stage 26 converts raw ss rows into one
# canonical endpoint per protocol/address/port/interface/network-namespace identity, merges all stable owners into
# a separate attribution identity, and keeps raw-row multiplicity as evidence. A bounded 45-day ledger records
# first/last seen, recurrence, and persistence. Review output is grouped by owner/purpose so repeated addresses and
# multi-owner sockets do not masquerade as independent policy decisions.
#
# Zones express intended trust and binding shape:
#   loopback     local-only services; exact local binding expected
#   admin-lan    administrative services; wildcard binding is evidence that future firewall gating is required
#   admin-public administrative SSH/ET explicitly permitted public reachability under later firewall enforcement
#   lan-services shared LAN services; wildcard binding likewise requires future source/interface filtering
#   public-p2p   intentionally public peer traffic; observation only until enforcement is separately reviewed
#   host-control host networking control-plane traffic such as DHCPv6
#   review       unresolved or deliberately operator-reviewed listeners
#
# `known` means recognized purpose. `review` means classification remains incomplete or operator-dependent.
# Neither means firewall permission. All records remain required=no so an application stopping does not make the
# machine unhealthy. Stage 27 will generate a firewall/service-binding dry run from this converged evidence; Stage
# 28 remains the separately reviewed enforcement stage.
#
# Grammar:
#   #@MNX-ZONE id=... interfaces=glob,... address=class,... bind_policy=exact|firewall-gated|any|review
#              sources=prefix,... enforcement=observe-only
#   #@MNX-EXPOSURE id=... proto=... port=... address=... interfaces=... netns=... unit=... process=...
#                  package=... cgroup=... mode=... disposition=known|review|forbidden intent=... zone=...
#                  required=no volatility=persistent|dynamic|transient
#
# MNX_EXPOSURE_POLICY_SCHEMA=2
# MNX_NETWORK_ZONE_SCHEMA=1
